# Product Naming — README

Structured naming that produces surprising, ownable names. Three phases in a loop:
**A: BRIEF** (understand context) → **B: SWARM** (17 generation exercises + 6-filter critic)
→ **C: FEEDBACK-LOOP** (iterate, preserve breadth).

> **Note:** this README is *not* read by the generator — it is documentation for you only.
> The skill itself (SKILL.md → brief.md → swarm.md → feedback.md) is intentionally **example-free**,
> so no names or domains from other tasks leak into your results. The examples below
> are here only to explain *what kind of name* each exercise builds.

---

## The 17 exercises — method and source

| # | Exercise | What it builds | Method / origin | Source |
|---|----------|----------------|-----------------|--------|
| 1 | Iterative Expansion | Compounds from core words | Associative expansion: explore concepts, not words | [S2] |
| 2 | Morphological Matrix | Systematic cross-combinations | Morphological analysis (Zwicky box) | [S4] |
| 3 | Provocation (PO) | Ideas from deliberately absurd statements | Lateral thinking, "Po" (Edward de Bono) | [S3] |
| 4 | Conceptual Spelunking | Ideas from over/under/side worlds | Ladder up / drill down / crawl across (A. Shore) | [S2] |
| 5 | Semantic Overlap | Words with double meaning | Suggestive/evocative names | [S1] |
| 6 | Action + Human | Warm word + human name | Human/warm compound (WelcomeBob-type) | [S1][S7] |
| 7 | Counter-Convention | Names that break category norms | Category differentiation | [S1][S8] |
| 8 | Random Stimulus | Ideas from a random word | Random entry (Edward de Bono) | [S3] |
| 9 | Etymology / Foreign Language | Short words from roots/languages | Classical roots, loanwords | [S1] |
| 10 | Five Whys → Root | Names from the deep cause | 5 Whys (Sakichi Toyoda / Toyota) | [S5] |
| 11 | Neologisms / Portmanteau | Constructed words | Coined names / portmanteau (Prowi-, Welvio-type) | [S1] |
| 12 | Sound Symbolism | Names designed on sound first | Phonetic sound symbolism (plosives, front/back vowels) | [S6][S7] |
| 13 | Metaphor Transfer | Image from a foreign world | Metaphor names + excursion technique | [S8][S2] |
| 14 | Borrowed Proper Noun | Myth/history/literature/geography | Archetype and mythology names (Jungian archetypes) | [S9] |
| 15 | Collocation Mining | Names from neighboring words/fixed phrases | Corpus/collocation method (A. Shore) | [S2] |
| 16 | Rhyme, Rhythm & Repetition | Names with a sonic hook | Phonetic repetition (rhyme, alliteration) | [S6] |
| 17 | Technical Term + Space/Container | Insider term + place word | Compound real-word names (GitHub-, GitLab-type) | [S1] |

---

## "Can it build a name like…?"

| Desired name type | Example archetype | Exercise(s) that build it |
|-------------------|-------------------|--------------------------|
| Warm word + human name | WelcomeBob | **6** (Action + Human) |
| Constructed/portmanteau word | Prowi, Welvio | **11** (Neologisms), supported by **12** (sound) |
| Technical term + space/container | GitHub, GitLab | **17** (Technical term + space), supported by **1, 2** |
| Repurposed everyday word in new context | (Oasis-type) | **5, 13** + Filter 5 (Repurposed-word test) |
| Borrowed proper noun with story | (myth/archetype) | **14** (Borrowed proper noun) |

Against **plain/boring** names, weight exercises **12 (sound), 13 (metaphor), 14 (borrowed meaning)
and 16 (rhythm)** — it is sound, image, and rhythm that make a name non-flat.

---

## Critic — 6 filters (all parameterized to the brief's category, no fixed examples)

0. **Quarantine** — discard names the user has already mentioned + variants
1. **Startup reality** — can a startup own it without a large budget?
2. **Category break** — would nobody in the brief's category call something this?
3. **Non-explanation** — does a stranger understand what kind of product it is?
4. **Surprise** — does the name have a reason to exist beyond pure logic?
5. **Repurposed word** — is it a known word planted in a new context?

---

## Two variants

- **`product-naming`** (this one): sequential, runs in ordinary chat. Generator runs all
  17 exercises internally in `references/swarm.md`.
- **`product-naming-parallel`**: spawns the 17 exercises as parallel subagents via
  `claude` CLI. Only for Claude Code. Each exercise is a separate file in `references/exercises/`.

---

## Sources

- **[S1]** How Brands Are Built — *Every type of brand name in one simple chart* (name taxonomy: descriptive/suggestive/abstract/coined/compound). https://howbrandsarebuilt.com/types-of-brand-names/
- **[S2]** Anthony Shore, Operative Words — *How to Name: Explore Concepts, not Words* (ladder up/drill down/crawl across, excursion, corpus/collocation method). https://www.operativewords.com/blog/2018/6/12/3kxk51nliieab9jxsss3xwg9vas7w0
- **[S3]** Edward de Bono — *Lateral Thinking* (provocation/"Po" and random entry). https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lateral_thinking
- **[S4]** Fritz Zwicky — *General Morphological Analysis / Zwicky box* (systematic combination). https://nesslabs.com/zwicky-box
- **[S5]** Sakichi Toyoda / Toyota — *Five Whys* (root cause). https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Five_whys
- **[S6]** *Sounds good: Phonetic sound patterns in top brand names* (sound symbolism research). https://www.researchgate.net/publication/285416618_Sounds_good_Phonetic_sound_patterns_in_top_brand_names
- **[S7]** The Identity Bureau — *Using sound symbolism to influence perceptions of brand names*. https://www.theidbureau.com/blog/using-sound-symbolism
- **[S8]** Tungsten Branding — *Why Metaphors Make Great Company Brand Names*. https://www.tungstenbranding.com/why-metaphors-make-great-company-brand-names/
- **[S9]** Sticky Branding — *The Gods Must Be Brands: How Brands Draw their Names from Greek Mythology*. https://stickybranding.com/blog/the-gods-must-be-brands-how-brands-draw-their-names-from-greek-mythology
